  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Smart adaptive sustainable horticulture</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project aims to develop practical and sustainable from low-tech, low-value protected to mid-tech and high-tech greenhouse systems for specific conditions in Algeria, Rwanda and South Africa.The project will reach this goal by adapting and adjusting the extensive high-tech knowledge, technology and entrepreneurship of the Dutch Horticulture sector to produce higher-quality vegetables in the selected countries.The approach is based on the so-called Smart Adaptive Sustainable Horticulture (SMASH) concept of Greenhouse Holland International (GHI), Wageningen University &amp; Research (WUR) and TNO, in combination with value chain development.The final goal is to generate solutions that match the local demand of investors. The applicant is Holland Horti International, a private partner with Cevital SPA, a major food producer and processor from Algeria for the high-tech variety, and Klein Karoo from South Africa for the med-tech solution. The project involves different partners who bring their expertise to the table. WUR and DLO specialize in plant production, specifically when it comes to tomato plants. TNO Greenhouse Horticulture offers knowledge of the modular greenhouse system, known as the System Integration Optimization Model (SIOM). BoP Innovation Centre is committed to creating inclusive innovations for lower-resource farmers, providing access to financial instruments and impact investors. At present, there are no local public partners involved in the project.The selection of a partner in Rwanda will take place as soon as possible. The main activities are expert support and training. The project will focus on tomato production in all three countries.</narrative>
  </description>
